            <ArticleTitle>Drug-seeking behavior in urolithiasis in the noncontrast computed tomography era: 2 cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>744</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Symptomatic urolithiasis is usually treated with narcotic pain management. This leads to the potential for use of its symptoms for personal gain. Historically, the typical presentation of a narcotic-seeking &quot;stone patient&quot; was a history of radiolucent stones and an intravenous contrast allergy. With the increased use of non-contrast-enhanced computed tomography to evaluate patients suspected of having acute urolithiasis, we have seen a change in the strategy of narcotic-seeking patients. We report 2 patients with pelvic calcifications on non-contrast-enhanced computed tomography feigning symptoms of urolithiasis to receive narcotic drugs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
